    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A heat pump commissioning record has to stand up to an MCS audit and a Boiler Upgrade Scheme claim. It ties the heat loss design, flow temperatures, balancing and controls into one handover pack. The aim is simple: the checklist is written while the installer is still on site with the system running.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>An MCS heat pump install must meet the MCS installation standards.</li><li>MCS 020 covers the noise check for where the outdoor unit goes.</li><li>Commissioning records the heat loss design, flow and return temperatures, balancing, refrigerant and electrical checks, and controls.</li><li>Boiler Upgrade Scheme grants in England and Wales rely on an MCS-certified install and its proof.</li><li>Software records the checklist and writes the report. It does not replace MCS certification, a competent installer, or F-Gas skills.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The point",
      "heading": "What heat pump commissioning software is for",
      "html": "<p>The design must be right first. A room-by-room heat loss calculation. Emitters sized for a low flow temperature. The outdoor unit sited and noise-checked under MCS 020. Commissioning then shows the system matches the design.</p><p>Software does not make that valid. The MCS installer does. Software makes the checklist easy to finish on site.</p>"
    },
